/S /H1
/T (Grinding \(bruxism\))
/E (Grinding \(bruxism\))
/Pg 80 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
2222 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (As you learned in chapters 4, grinding can be result from an undiagnosed airway issue, occlusal disease, or true bruxism \(which is only the case for about 8% of people diagnosed with grinding\). Unfortunately, true bruxism has no real solution as it is a neurological issue. )
/Pg 80 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
2223 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Some helpful tips are to first wear a nightguard every night to minimize damage to the teeth and distribute the force evenly. I recommend having one custom made by a dentist. Learning to calm your nervous system with meditation, relaxing nighttime rituals, herbal remedies and supplements, etc could all be helpful in preparing your brain to sleep in peace. As mentioned above, grinding leads to gum recession because of the excessive stress concentrated at the junction of the tooth and the jaw bone. This stress causes bone loss and gum recession. Please refer to chapter 3 for more on resolving airway-related grinding and chapter 4 for occlusal disease.)
/Pg 80 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
